== Why does it happen?
OAS occurs in some people who are allergic to certain proteins found in plant pollens (like trees, grass, or weed pollen). *Certain fresh fruits, vegetables, and tree nuts contain proteins that look very similar to the plant pollen protein*. When you eat these specific fresh foods, your immune system gets confused - _it thinks you have eaten a mouthful of plant pollen_! This leads to usually a mild, quick-onset, and self-limited localized reaction - commonly itching, tingling, or mild swelling of the lips, mouth, and throat.

== Why Raw Foods Cause Reactions, but Cooked Foods Do Not
You might notice that you can eat a slice of apple pie without any issues, but eating a raw apple makes your mouth itch. *The proteins responsible for OAS are very fragile*. When the food is heated or heavily processed, the proteins break down and are no longer recognized by your immune system.

Stomach acid also breaks down these fragile proteins quickly, which is why symptoms are usually limited to the mouth and throat and very rarely progress to severe, whole-body reactions like anaphylaxis.

== Can I still eat the fresh foods that cause me symptoms?

*Ideally, you should avoid the raw/fresh foods that cause you symptoms*. While symptoms of OAS are mild, localized, and self limited the vast majority of the time, a small percentage of people may develop a severe reaction called anaphylaxis.

Eating cooked or processed versions as tolerated is encouraged.

